from authoring import make, Q
LESSONS=[]
LESSONS.append(make(
1,'Introducing Yourself Professionally','Профессиональное знакомство','professional introductions',
'''academic support | академическая поддержка | A university service that helps with academic procedures. | I work in academic support and coordinate student requests.
coordinate | координировать | To organise people or tasks so that work happens smoothly. | I coordinate communication between students and departments.
responsibility | обязанность | A duty that belongs to someone\'s role. | One of my responsibilities is checking requests.
liaise with | поддерживать связь с | To communicate between people or groups about shared work. | We liaise with the international office.
assist | помогать | To provide practical help or guidance. | I assist students with academic documents.
colleague | коллега | Someone you work with. | My colleague manages scheduling enquiries.
enquiry | запрос | A question asking for information. | We receive enquiries about course registration.
department | отдел | A division of an organisation with specific functions. | Our department supports academic operations.
point of contact | контактное лицо | The person who should receive a question or request. | I will be your main point of contact.
follow up | уточнять позже; продолжать работу | To contact someone again after an earlier exchange. | I will follow up by email after the meeting.''',
'''It\'s a pleasure to meet you. | Рад(а) познакомиться.
I work in the Academic Support Department. | Я работаю в отделе академической поддержки.
My role involves coordinating student enquiries. | Моя работа включает координацию запросов студентов.
I mainly deal with academic administration. | В основном я занимаюсь академическим администрированием.
I\'m your point of contact for this matter. | По этому вопросу вы можете обращаться ко мне.
Please feel free to get in touch. | Смело обращайтесь ко мне.''',
'A first meeting with a visiting coordinator',
'''On Monday morning, the Academic Support team welcomes a visiting coordinator from a partner university. The coordinator has never met the local staff, so the first conversation is an opportunity to establish clear roles. A short introduction is more useful than a long speech about the university. The support officer says their name, states their department, and explains two responsibilities: answering student enquiries and coordinating academic documents. They also introduce a colleague who manages timetables.
The visitor asks whether the officer can approve a student\'s request to change a module. Instead of promising something outside their authority, the officer explains that a programme coordinator makes the final decision. The support team can check whether the request is complete and forward it to the right person. This is a useful example of professional boundaries. You can be helpful without claiming responsibility for every decision.
Before the conversation ends, the officer offers to send contact details and a short summary by email. The visitor now knows whom to approach, what the support team can do, and what happens next. A successful introduction creates trust because it is brief, specific and accurate. In international university work, clarity is often more valuable than complicated vocabulary.''',
'''Visitor | Good morning. I\'m Maya Chen, the visiting programme coordinator. It\'s lovely to meet you.
Officer | Welcome, Maya. I\'m Alex, and I work in the Academic Support Department.
Visitor | Could you tell me what your team usually handles?
Officer | Certainly. We answer academic enquiries, check supporting documents and liaise with programme teams.
Visitor | Can you approve a change of module as well?
Officer | The programme coordinator makes that decision. We can explain the steps and forward a complete request.
Visitor | That helps. Who should I contact about scheduling?
Officer | My colleague handles timetables. I\'ll introduce you and send both contact details after our meeting.''',
('Present Simple for roles','Present Simple — обязанности','Use the Present Simple for regular work and stable responsibilities. Use “I work in…” for a department and “I am responsible for…” for a defined task.','I work in academic support.;My team handles student enquiries.;I am responsible for coordinating requests.','Avoid “I am work”; say “I work” or “I am working” in a genuinely temporary context.',Q('Which sentence correctly describes a regular responsibility?','I coordinate academic requests.','I am coordinate academic requests.','I coordinating academic requests.','Regular responsibilities use the Present Simple.')),
[Q('Who makes the final decision about a module change?','The programme coordinator.','The visiting coordinator.','The student support officer.','The officer explicitly states that the programme coordinator decides.'),Q('Why does the officer offer to send an email?','To confirm contact details and next steps.','To ask for a new job.','To cancel the meeting.','The follow-up makes responsibilities and communication channels clear.')],
[Q('What does the support department check?','Supporting documents.','Building maintenance invoices.','Travel insurance policies.','The officer lists supporting documents among the team’s tasks.'),Q('What does Alex promise to do?','Introduce a colleague and email contact details.','Approve a module change immediately.','Rewrite the programme handbook.','Listen for the two specific follow-up actions.')],
'Introduce yourself to a representative from a partner university. Explain two tasks you handle and one task that requires another department.',
'You are meeting a new visiting lecturer. Introduce your role, explain your responsibilities, and offer a next step.',
'It is a pleasure to meet you. I work in academic support, where I answer student enquiries and help coordinate academic documents. If a decision needs approval, I contact the relevant programme team. I would be happy to send you our contact details.',
'Write a 70–100-word introductory email to a new international colleague. State your role, two responsibilities and how they can reach you.',
'Dear Maya, It was a pleasure meeting you today. I work in the Academic Support Department and primarily assist with student enquiries and academic documentation. For programme decisions, I liaise with the relevant academic coordinator. Please feel free to contact me with any questions, and I will be glad to direct you to the appropriate person. Kind regards, Alex',
('I am work in the academic department.','I work in the academic department.','Use the Present Simple for a regular professional role.'),
Q('A visitor assumes you can approve every request. What is the best reply?','I can explain the process, but final approval comes from the programme coordinator.','Of course, I will approve everything today.','That is not my problem.','Acknowledge the request while stating your actual authority.')))
LESSONS.append(make(
2,'Professional Small Talk','Непринуждённый разговор','professional small talk',
'''make conversation | поддерживать беседу | To exchange friendly remarks, often before work begins. | We made conversation while waiting for the session.
settle in | освоиться | To become comfortable in a new place or role. | Have you had time to settle in?
visit | визит | A period spent in another place for a purpose. | Is this your first visit to our campus?
local area | окрестности | The neighbourhood around a location. | Have you explored the local area?
recommend | рекомендовать | To suggest something as a good choice. | I can recommend a café nearby.
travel arrangements | организация поездки | Plans for transport and accommodation. | Were your travel arrangements straightforward?
weather forecast | прогноз погоды | A prediction of upcoming weather. | The weather forecast looks pleasant this week.
conversation starter | начало беседы | A safe question or comment that opens a conversation. | The campus tour was an easy conversation starter.
follow someone\'s lead | подстраиваться под собеседника | To respond to the topics and tone another person chooses. | Follow the guest\'s lead if they prefer to discuss work.
wrap up | завершать | To end something politely and efficiently. | We wrapped up our conversation before the seminar.''',
'''How was your journey? | Как прошла дорога?
Is this your first time visiting our campus? | Вы впервые у нас в кампусе?
Have you had a chance to explore the city? | Вам удалось посмотреть город?
I hope you\'re settling in well. | Надеюсь, вы уже освоились.
That sounds interesting. Tell me more. | Звучит интересно. Расскажите подробнее.
Shall we head to the meeting room? | Может, пройдём в переговорную?''',
'Before the international staff workshop',
'''A group of university employees is waiting for a workshop to begin. Several guests have travelled from overseas campuses, and the Academic Support officer wants everyone to feel welcome. The first exchange should be friendly, but it should not feel like an interview. The officer asks one visitor how their journey went and listens to the answer. When the guest says that the flight was long, a short sympathetic response is appropriate. There is no need to ask for private details.
Another visitor mentions that they have never visited the city before. The officer offers a simple recommendation: a quiet café near campus. The visitor asks if it is within walking distance. Now the conversation has developed naturally from something the guest said. Questions about food, travel or the event itself often work well in an international workplace. Questions about salary, family plans or personal beliefs may feel intrusive, especially when people have just met.
As the workshop begins, the officer closes the conversation: “It was lovely speaking with you. Shall we take our seats?” This ending sounds warm and professional. Small talk is not about being entertaining. It helps people relax, notice common interests and make future work conversations easier. The best strategy is to listen, respond, and allow the other person to choose how much they want to share.''',
'''Officer | Welcome to the workshop. How was your journey?
Guest | Thank you. The flight was a little delayed, but everything else went well.
Officer | I\'m glad you arrived safely. Is this your first time here?
Guest | Yes, and I would love to see the city after the workshop.
Officer | There is a lovely walking area near campus. I can share a few recommendations.
Guest | That would be great. Have you worked at this university long?
Officer | Just over two years. I mainly support academic administration.
Guest | Interesting! I\'d like to hear more. Oh, I think the session is starting.
Officer | Of course. It was lovely chatting. Shall we head inside?''',
('Present Perfect for experiences','Present Perfect — опыт','Use “Have you ever…?” or “Have you had a chance to…?” for experience up to now. Use the Past Simple when a specific finished time is given.','Have you visited the campus before?;Did you arrive yesterday?;I have worked here for two years.','Do not say “Have you arrived yesterday?” because yesterday is a finished time.',Q('Which question naturally asks about experience?','Have you visited the city before?','Did you ever visited the city?','Have you visit the city before?','Present Perfect uses have/has plus past participle.')),
[Q('Which topic is identified as potentially intrusive?','Personal salary.','The campus workshop.','A nearby café.','The reading cautions against asking for private information.'),Q('What makes the conversation develop naturally?','Listening and responding to something the guest said.','Asking as many questions as possible.','Talking only about yourself.','Follow the other person’s lead rather than using an interrogation list.')],
[Q('What problem did the guest face?','A delayed flight.','Lost academic records.','A cancelled workshop.','The guest describes a slight flight delay.'),Q('What does the guest want to do later?','Explore the city.','Apply for admission.','Lead the workshop.','The guest says they would love to see the city.')],
'Welcome an overseas colleague before a meeting. Keep a friendly two-minute conversation going and end it politely.',
'You meet a visiting professor outside a workshop. Ask two safe questions and respond thoughtfully to one answer.',
'Welcome to our campus! How was your journey? I am glad the trip went well. Is this your first visit to the city? If you have time after the workshop, I can recommend a nice place nearby. The session is about to begin, so shall we go inside?',
'Write a short, warm message to welcome a visiting lecturer who arrives tomorrow. Include useful information without being intrusive.',
'Hello Dr Rivera, We are looking forward to welcoming you tomorrow. The workshop begins at 10:00 in Building C, Room 204. Please let me know if you need directions or any practical information before your visit. I hope you have a comfortable journey. Best wishes, Alex',
('Have you visited us yesterday?','Did you visit us yesterday?','Use Past Simple with a completed past time such as yesterday.'),
Q('A guest answers your travel question briefly and looks at the schedule. What should you do?','Follow their lead and move towards the workshop.','Ask several more personal questions.','Insist on telling a long travel story.','Effective small talk respects the other person’s cues.')))
LESSONS.append(make(
3,'Describing Your Responsibilities','Объяснение своих обязанностей','department roles and workflows',
'''day-to-day | повседневный | Relating to routine work done regularly. | My day-to-day tasks include checking enquiries.
workflow | рабочий процесс | A series of steps used to complete a task. | We follow a clear workflow for academic requests.
process a request | обработать запрос | To complete administrative steps needed for a request. | We process routine document requests within the stated period.
refer to | направить к | To send a case or person to the appropriate specialist. | We refer complex policy questions to the academic office.
escalate | передать на более высокий уровень | To pass an issue to someone with greater authority. | We escalate unusual cases to the department lead.
responsible for | отвечающий за | Having a defined duty. | The registrar is responsible for official records.
collaborate | сотрудничать | To work with others on a shared task. | We collaborate with faculty administrators.
verify | проверять достоверность | To check whether information is accurate. | We verify the documents before forwarding them.
turnaround time | срок обработки | The expected time to complete a service. | The turnaround time depends on the request type.
within our remit | в пределах полномочий отдела | Within the scope of a team\'s responsibilities. | General guidance is within our remit.''',
'''My day-to-day work includes… | Моя повседневная работа включает…
Our team is responsible for… | Наша команда отвечает за…
We usually handle requests related to… | Обычно мы обрабатываем запросы по…
That falls under another department. | Это относится к другому отделу.
Let me refer you to the right colleague. | Позвольте направить вас к нужному коллеге.
The usual process has three steps. | Обычная процедура состоит из трёх этапов.''',
'Who handles which academic request?',
'''A partner university sends several questions to one general email address. One question concerns a student\'s transcript, another concerns a change to a programme, and a third asks about a teaching timetable. All three are academic questions, but they do not necessarily belong to the same team. The Academic Support officer begins by separating the requests according to their purpose. This prevents unnecessary delays and inaccurate promises.
The support team can explain the process for obtaining documents, confirm which forms are required, and check whether a request has all the necessary information. The registrar or records office may issue the official transcript. Programme changes, however, usually require an academic decision from an authorised coordinator. The timetable team may control room allocation and teaching schedules. The officer should verify local procedures before giving an exact timeframe.
When explaining responsibilities to a new colleague, clear verbs are more useful than broad labels. Saying “We help students” is correct but vague. Saying “We check documents, track requests, and refer policy questions to authorised colleagues” gives a clearer picture. It is also important to distinguish between handling a case and approving it. An employee can coordinate a process without having the authority to change the underlying academic decision. Good explanations help partners contact the right team and understand realistic next steps.''',
'''Partner | Could you explain how academic requests are managed at your university?
Officer | Of course. Our team receives enquiries, checks the basic information and directs them to the relevant unit.
Partner | So you issue official transcripts?
Officer | We explain the requirements and track the request, but the records office issues official transcripts.
Partner | What about a student\'s programme change?
Officer | That decision belongs to the authorised programme team. We can help the student submit the request correctly.
Partner | How long does the entire process take?
Officer | It depends on the request type. I can verify the published turnaround time and send it to you.''',
('Present Simple + sequencing','Обязанности и последовательность','Use the Present Simple for routines. Use first, then and finally to make a workflow easy to follow.','First, we check the form.;Then, we refer the case to the appropriate team.;Finally, we confirm the next step.','Avoid promising a precise deadline without checking institutional policy.',Q('Choose the best routine-process sentence.','First, we verify the documents.','First, we verifying the documents.','First, we verified usually the documents.','A routine process takes the Present Simple.')),
[Q('Who issues the official transcript in this example?','The records office.','Academic Support alone.','The visiting university.','The reading distinguishes guidance from issuing official records.'),Q('Why does the officer divide the questions?','Different teams have different authority.','All questions are answered by students.','To avoid responding to the partner.','Correct routing reduces errors and delays.')],
[Q('What can Academic Support do with a programme change?','Help submit the request correctly.','Approve the academic decision.','Change the programme automatically.','The officer explicitly distinguishes coordination from approval.'),Q('What determines processing time?','The type of request.','The visitor’s country alone.','The officer’s personal preference.','The officer says it depends on the request type.')],
'Explain a three-step document request workflow and clarify who has final authority.',
'A new international colleague asks what you handle and what another office handles. Give a clear 45-second explanation.',
'Our team receives academic enquiries, checks basic documents and coordinates follow-up. For official transcripts, we explain the requirements, but the records office issues the document. For programme changes, the academic team makes the decision. We can help you reach the appropriate colleague.',
'Write a short departmental overview for an international staff handbook. Name three duties and one boundary.',
'The Academic Support Department provides guidance on academic enquiries, checks submitted information and coordinates communication with specialist teams. It does not independently approve programme changes or issue every official record. Where necessary, staff direct requests to the authorised unit and explain the next steps.',
('Our team responsible for student enquiries.','Our team is responsible for student enquiries.','“Responsible for” is an adjective phrase and needs the verb be.'),
Q('A colleague asks your team to sign off an academic decision. What is appropriate?','Explain who is authorised and offer to coordinate the request.','Sign the decision to save time.','Ignore the question.','Professional boundary-setting is part of accurate communication.')))
LESSONS.append(make(
4,'Asking Questions & Clarifying Information','Уточняющие вопросы','clarifying and confirming information',
'''clarify | прояснять | To make something easier to understand. | Could you clarify which form you mean?
confirm | подтвердить | To check that something is correct. | Can I confirm the submission date?
misunderstanding | недоразумение | A situation where a message is understood incorrectly. | We resolved the misunderstanding by checking the reference number.
rephrase | переформулировать | To say the same idea in different words. | Could you rephrase your last point?
specific | конкретный | Clearly identified and precise. | Do you have a specific deadline in mind?
reference number | регистрационный номер | A code used to identify a case or request. | Please include your reference number in the email.
spell out | произнести по буквам | To say the individual letters of a word. | Could you spell out the surname?
interpret | интерпретировать | To understand the intended meaning. | I may have interpreted the instruction differently.
recap | кратко подытожить | To summarise the main points. | Let me recap our next steps.
get back to | ответить позже | To contact someone later with an answer. | I\'ll get back to you after I check.''',
'''Could you clarify what you mean by…? | Не могли бы вы уточнить, что имеете в виду под…?
Would you mind repeating the last part? | Не могли бы вы повторить последнюю часть?
Let me check that I\'ve understood. | Позвольте проверить, правильно ли я понял(а).
Am I right in thinking that…? | Правильно ли я понимаю, что…?
Could you give me a specific example? | Не могли бы вы привести конкретный пример?
I\'ll get back to you once I\'ve confirmed it. | Я отвечу, когда уточню информацию.''',
'Clarifying a registration question on a call',
'''An international coordinator calls the Academic Support office about a registration problem. The connection is clear, but the coordinator speaks quickly and uses a programme abbreviation that the officer does not recognise. It would be risky to guess the meaning. A polite clarification question is a normal part of professional communication, especially when two institutions use different terms.
The officer asks the caller to repeat the programme title and spell the abbreviation. Then the officer checks whether the student needs initial registration or a change to an existing registration. These are different procedures. Instead of repeating the caller\'s sentence word for word, the officer summarises the key facts in plain English: the student has already enrolled, but a module does not appear on the current timetable. The caller confirms that this is correct.
The officer still does not know why the timetable is incomplete. Rather than inventing an answer, they explain that they will contact the appropriate unit and follow up by email. Before ending the call, both people agree on the correct email address and reference number. A short recap saves time later because it creates a shared understanding of the situation. Asking for clarification is not evidence of weak English; it is a professional strategy for preventing mistakes.''',
'''Coordinator | I\'m calling about the PGD registration issue. The timetable seems incomplete.
Officer | Thank you. Could you spell out the programme abbreviation for me?
Coordinator | Yes, P-G-D. The student is already enrolled, but one module is missing.
Officer | Let me check that I\'ve understood. The enrollment is complete, but the module is not on the timetable. Is that right?
Coordinator | Exactly. We\'re trying to establish whether anything needs to be submitted.
Officer | I don\'t want to give you inaccurate information. I\'ll check with the timetable team.
Coordinator | That would be helpful. Our reference number is AC-184.
Officer | Thank you. I\'ll include AC-184 in my follow-up email and confirm the next step.''',
('Indirect questions','Вежливые косвенные вопросы','Use indirect questions to sound professional: “Could you tell me when the meeting starts?” Use statement word order after the introductory phrase.','When does the meeting start?;Could you tell me when the meeting starts?;I was wondering whether the form is complete.','Avoid “Could you tell me when does the meeting start?”',Q('Which indirect question has correct word order?','Could you tell me when the meeting starts?','Could you tell me when does the meeting start?','Could you tell me when starts the meeting?','After an indirect-question opener, use statement word order.')),
[Q('What is the actual problem with the student record?','One module is missing from the timetable.','The student has never enrolled.','The student has lost a passport.','The officer confirms that enrollment is complete but the timetable is incomplete.'),Q('Why does the officer avoid guessing?','Different institutions may use different terms.','Guessing is always faster.','The officer wants to end the call.','Clarifying unfamiliar terminology prevents incorrect guidance.')],
[Q('What reference number does the caller give?','AC-184.','AC-148.','PGD-814.','Listen for the short alphanumeric reference.'),Q('What will the officer do next?','Check with the timetable team and email an update.','Approve a new module immediately.','Ask the student to apply again.','The next step is verification and a documented follow-up.')],
'Handle a fast-paced call about a missing module. Ask three clarifying questions before offering the next step.',
'A partner speaks quickly and uses an unfamiliar abbreviation. Ask for clarification without sounding apologetic or insecure.',
'Could you spell out that abbreviation, please? Let me check that I have understood: the student is enrolled, but one module is missing from the timetable. Is that correct? I will verify the details and get back to you by email.',
'Write a follow-up email that restates a caller’s problem, asks for one missing detail and promises a reasonable next action.',
'Dear Colleague, Thank you for your call. As discussed, the student is enrolled, but one module is not visible on the timetable. Could you confirm the module code? I will check the case with the timetable team and update you when I receive clarification. Kind regards, Alex',
('Could you tell me where is the academic office?','Could you tell me where the academic office is?','Embedded questions use statement word order.'),
Q('You cannot understand a programme abbreviation. What should you do?','Ask for the full name and confirm your understanding.','Pretend to understand to keep the conversation moving.','Assume it refers to postgraduate admissions.','A short clarification is safer than an inaccurate answer.')))
LESSONS.append(make(
5,'Everyday University Communication','Повседневное общение в университете','daily coordination and polite requests',
'''available | доступный; свободный | Free for a meeting or task. | Are you available for a quick call?
arrange | организовывать | To make practical plans for an event or activity. | I can arrange a meeting with the coordinator.
reschedule | перенести | To change an agreed time or date. | We need to reschedule tomorrow\'s briefing.
notify | уведомлять | To formally tell someone about a change. | Please notify the participants about the new room.
urgent | срочный | Needing prompt attention. | This request is urgent because the deadline is today.
attachment | вложение | A file sent together with an email. | The attachment contains the updated timetable.
prioritise | расставлять приоритеты | To decide what should receive attention first. | We prioritise time-sensitive student issues.
update | обновление | New information about a process. | I will send an update this afternoon.
check in | связываться для уточнения | To contact someone briefly to see how things are going. | I wanted to check in about the document.
pass on | передавать информацию | To give information to another person. | Could you pass on the message to your team?''',
'''Would you be available for a short call? | Вы могли бы найти время для короткого звонка?
Could you please send the updated file? | Не могли бы вы прислать обновлённый файл?
I\'ll notify everyone about the change. | Я уведомлю всех об изменении.
Let me check the schedule first. | Дайте сначала свериться с расписанием.
Thanks for bringing this to my attention. | Спасибо, что обратили на это моё внимание.
I\'ll keep you updated. | Я буду держать вас в курсе.''',
'A normal morning in Academic Support',
'''An Academic Support officer begins the day with four messages. A lecturer requests a new meeting time. A student asks where to submit a form. A colleague reports that a room has changed, and an international partner asks for the latest timetable. These tasks are connected to different people, but all need clear and timely communication. The officer checks which questions are urgent before replying.
The student\'s form has a deadline that afternoon, so the officer replies with the relevant submission instructions first. The room change affects a class later that day, so the officer confirms the new location and notifies the participants. The lecturer\'s meeting can be rescheduled after checking the shared calendar. Finally, the updated timetable is sent to the partner with a note explaining which version is current. Even a short message should state what changed and what the recipient needs to do.
Small professional phrases help the whole morning run more smoothly. “Thanks for bringing this to my attention” acknowledges a problem without blaming anyone. “Let me check the schedule first” avoids making a promise too early. “I\'ll keep you updated” signals that further information will follow. These expressions are useful because they are polite, specific and practical. Effective daily communication is less about complicated grammar than about helping other people act on accurate information.''',
'''Lecturer | Good morning. Could we move our briefing to Thursday?
Officer | Certainly. Let me check the shared calendar first.
Lecturer | Thanks. I also heard the workshop room may have changed.
Officer | Yes, it has moved to Room 312. I\'ll notify everyone today.
Lecturer | Could you send me the updated timetable as well?
Officer | Of course. I\'ll attach the latest version to my email.
Lecturer | One of my students needs instructions for a form due today.
Officer | That\'s time-sensitive, so I\'ll send the student the submission guidance first. I\'ll keep you updated.''',
('Modal verbs for polite requests','Модальные глаголы для просьб','Could and would soften requests. “Could you…?” asks about action; “Would you be available…?” asks about willingness or availability.','Could you send the attachment?;Would you be available on Thursday?;I can arrange a short call.','Avoid making routine requests sound like commands: “Send this now!”',Q('Choose the most polite request.','Could you please send the updated timetable?','Send updated timetable immediately.','You must to send the timetable.','Could you please…? is natural and courteous.')),
[Q('Which request is most time-sensitive?','The form due that afternoon.','The meeting next Thursday.','A general question about a future course.','The short deadline makes the form urgent.'),Q('What should an update message explain?','What changed and what the recipient should do.','Only the sender’s name.','Why everyone else is wrong.','Specific changes and next steps make updates useful.')],
[Q('Where has the workshop moved?','Room 312.','Room 321.','Room 231.','The officer states Room 312.'),Q('What will be attached to the email?','The latest timetable.','An admissions contract.','A completed exam.','The officer offers to attach the latest version.')],
'Handle four short requests in the right order: a deadline, room change, meeting request and partner update.',
'Explain politely how you would prioritise requests from a student, lecturer and partner university.',
'I would reply to the student with the urgent deadline first. Next, I would confirm the room change and notify everyone affected. Then I could check availability for the lecturer’s meeting and send the current timetable to our partner.',
'Draft a concise message informing students about a changed room and asking them to check the updated timetable.',
'Dear Students, Please note that today’s workshop will take place in Room 312 instead of Room 210. The start time is unchanged. Kindly check the updated timetable attached and contact Academic Support if anything is unclear. Thank you for your understanding. Best regards, Academic Support',
('Could you to send the attachment?','Could you send the attachment?','After modal could, use the base form of the verb without to.'),
Q('Two requests arrive: an afternoon deadline and a meeting next week. What is the best first step?','Address the time-sensitive deadline and communicate clearly about the meeting.','Ignore both until the next morning.','Promise to solve everything without checking details.','Prioritise based on urgency and effect on users.')))
LESSONS.append(make(
6,'Academic Programs & Degrees','Академические программы и степени','programmes, modules and academic pathways',
'''undergraduate | бакалавриат | Study leading to a first university degree. | The undergraduate programme normally lasts several years.
postgraduate | последипломный уровень | Study after an undergraduate degree. | This postgraduate course requires a relevant qualification.
degree programme | программа получения степени | A structured course of study leading to a degree. | Students choose a degree programme during admission.
module | учебный модуль | A unit within a programme, sometimes called a course. | The module introduces research methods.
credit | академический кредит | A measure of study workload or achievement. | The number of credits varies by institution.
compulsory | обязательный | Required as part of a programme. | Research skills is a compulsory module.
elective | дисциплина по выбору | A subject chosen from available options. | Students can select an elective in semester two.
curriculum | учебная программа | The planned subjects and learning experiences in a programme. | The curriculum is reviewed periodically.
prerequisite | предварительное требование | Something that must be completed before another activity. | Statistics is a prerequisite for the advanced module.
academic pathway | образовательная траектория | A planned sequence of study choices and stages. | The adviser explained the available academic pathways.''',
'''This programme consists of six core modules. | Эта программа состоит из шести основных модулей.
Students must complete the prerequisites. | Студенты должны выполнить предварительные требования.
Credit systems vary between institutions. | Системы кредитов различаются между университетами.
This subject is optional rather than compulsory. | Этот предмет факультативный, а не обязательный.
Let me explain how the modules fit together. | Позвольте объяснить, как связаны модули.
The programme team can confirm equivalence. | Команда программы может подтвердить эквивалентность.''',
'Explaining a study plan to a visiting adviser',
'''A visiting adviser from another university wants to understand the structure of a local degree programme. The Academic Support officer begins with a simple overview: the programme is divided into semesters and includes compulsory modules as well as elective options. Each module has its own learning outcomes and assessment requirements. These details help students plan their workload, but the precise meaning of a credit is not necessarily the same at every institution.
The adviser asks whether students can choose an advanced research module in their first semester. The officer checks the programme guide and explains that a foundation module is a prerequisite. This is an academic requirement, not a personal preference. The officer also explains that modules sometimes have different names even when the subject matter is similar. Therefore, the officer should not promise that credits will transfer automatically between universities. Equivalence must be reviewed by an authorised academic team.
At the end of the discussion, the adviser requests a written overview of the curriculum. The officer agrees to share the publicly approved programme guide and provide contact information for the programme team. This exchange shows how to communicate complex academic information without making unsupported claims. A good explanation starts with the overall structure, defines unfamiliar terms, and then addresses the visitor\'s specific question.''',
'''Adviser | Could you explain how your undergraduate programme is organised?
Officer | Certainly. It is divided into semesters, with compulsory modules and several electives.
Adviser | Can students take Advanced Research in their first semester?
Officer | Not usually. They must complete Foundation Research first because it is a prerequisite.
Adviser | Are your credits automatically recognised by partner universities?
Officer | Credit systems vary. The authorised academic team needs to review equivalence.
Adviser | Could you send me a programme overview?
Officer | Yes. I\'ll share the approved guide and the contact for the programme team.''',
('Must, need to and can','Требования и возможности','Use must or need to for requirements, can for available options, and may for possible exceptions. Avoid presenting institutional examples as universal rules.','Students must complete the prerequisite.;Students can choose an elective.;Some exceptions may apply.','Do not confuse “must” (requirement) with “can” (possibility).',Q('Which sentence clearly expresses a requirement?','Students must complete the prerequisite.','Students can complete the prerequisite if they want.','Students might maybe completed the prerequisite.','Must signals a required condition.')),
[Q('Why should the officer not promise automatic credit transfer?','Credit equivalence requires authorised review.','All institutions use identical credit systems.','Electives never receive credits.','Institutional credit systems and decisions vary.'),Q('What is needed before Advanced Research?','Foundation Research.','A visa interview.','A student club membership.','The foundation module is described as the prerequisite.')],
[Q('What types of modules does the programme include?','Compulsory and elective.','Only compulsory.','Only informal workshops.','The officer names both categories.'),Q('Who reviews credit equivalence?','The authorised academic team.','The support officer alone.','Any student with a timetable.','The adviser is directed to the appropriate decision maker.')],
'Explain a programme with compulsory modules, electives and prerequisites to a partner adviser.',
'A visitor asks whether one of their students can transfer credits. Explain the basics and avoid promising approval.',
'The programme has compulsory modules and elective options. Students must complete any prerequisites before taking advanced modules. Because credit systems differ, I cannot confirm a transfer myself. The academic team can review the relevant module details.',
'Write a short explanation of module prerequisites to a student who wants to enrol in an advanced module.',
'Dear Student, Thank you for your question. Advanced Research requires successful completion of Foundation Research. Please check your academic record before submitting a registration request. If you believe you have completed an equivalent module elsewhere, contact the programme team for guidance on the review process. Kind regards, Academic Support',
('Students must to complete the prerequisite.','Students must complete the prerequisite.','A modal verb is followed by the bare infinitive.'),
Q('A partner asks if their credits will definitely transfer. Which reply is safest?','The academic team will need to review the module and credit details.','Yes, all credits transfer automatically.','The number of credits never matters.','Avoid making decisions for an authorised academic unit.')))
LESSONS.append(make(
7,'Admissions & Enrollment','Поступление и зачисление','admissions, eligibility and registration',
'''admission | приём в университет | Acceptance to study, following an application decision. | Admission decisions are made by the relevant admissions unit.
application | заявление; заявка | A formal request to join a programme. | The student submitted an application online.
eligibility | соответствие требованиям | Meeting the conditions to apply or take part. | The team checks eligibility against published criteria.
enrollment | зачисление; регистрация на обучение | The process of becoming officially registered to study. | Enrollment follows the acceptance of an offer.
supporting document | подтверждающий документ | Evidence included with an application or request. | A transcript may be a supporting document.
verify | проверять | To confirm the accuracy of information. | The office must verify identity information.
conditional offer | условное предложение о зачислении | An offer that depends on further conditions being met. | The student received a conditional offer pending a final result.
requirement | требование | A condition that needs to be met. | Language proficiency may be an entry requirement.
missing information | недостающие сведения | Information needed but not yet provided. | The file cannot be reviewed without the missing information.
registration status | статус регистрации | A record showing the stage of a registration process. | Please check your registration status in the portal.''',
'''Could you confirm your application number? | Не могли бы вы подтвердить номер заявки?
Your file appears to be missing one document. | Похоже, в вашем деле отсутствует один документ.
Admission and enrollment are separate stages. | Приём и регистрация — разные этапы.
Please check the published requirements. | Пожалуйста, проверьте опубликованные требования.
I can explain the next step in the process. | Я могу объяснить следующий этап процедуры.
The admissions team makes the final decision. | Окончательное решение принимает отдел приёма.''',
'An offer is not the same as enrollment',
'''An international student sends a message saying, “I received an offer, so I must already be enrolled.” The Academic Support officer needs to explain an important distinction. Admission generally concerns the decision to accept an applicant, while enrollment or registration concerns the formal steps required to become an active student. Universities use different portals and terminology, so the officer first checks the local guidance.
The student has received a conditional offer. One condition is to provide a final academic document. The officer explains that the student should read the conditions carefully and submit the required evidence through the designated process. Receiving an offer does not automatically mean that all enrollment steps are complete. The officer avoids asking the student to send sensitive documents through an unsecured chat. Instead, they direct the student to the institution\'s approved upload channel.
The officer also explains the limits of Academic Support: staff can describe published requirements and help identify missing information, but only an authorised admissions team can confirm whether an offer condition has been satisfied. The student appreciates the clear sequence of steps. Good admission-related communication uses short sentences, distinguishes similar terms and avoids promises about decisions that have not yet been made. It also respects confidentiality when handling student data.''',
'''Student | I received an offer yesterday. Does that mean I\'m fully enrolled now?
Officer | Congratulations on your offer. Admission and enrollment are separate steps at our university.
Student | My offer says it is conditional. What should I do?
Officer | Please check the conditions listed in your letter. It looks like the final academic document is still required.
Student | Can I send the document to you in this chat?
Officer | Please use the university\'s approved upload portal to protect your information.
Student | Who will confirm that the condition is complete?
Officer | The admissions team will review the evidence and confirm your status.''',
('Conditionals for procedures','Условные предложения для инструкций','Use if + Present Simple followed by will/can/imperative to explain conditional next steps. Keep statements institution-specific.','If a document is missing, the team will contact you.;If you have an offer, check its conditions.;You can ask for help if the instructions are unclear.','Avoid “If you will submit the form, we will review it” in an ordinary real-condition clause.',Q('Which sentence gives a natural condition?','If a document is missing, the team will contact you.','If a document will be missing, the team contact you.','If a missing document, team contact.','For likely conditions, use if + Present Simple.')),
[Q('What is the student mistaken about?','An offer does not necessarily mean enrollment is complete.','Conditional offers are always rejected.','Every programme uses one identical portal.','The distinction between admission and enrollment is the main issue.'),Q('Why should the document be uploaded through an approved channel?','To protect sensitive information.','To avoid all official review.','To share it publicly.','The reading emphasises confidentiality.')],
[Q('What document appears to be outstanding?','A final academic document.','A travel insurance receipt.','A parking permit.','The student must satisfy an academic-document condition.'),Q('Who confirms whether the condition is satisfied?','The admissions team.','The academic support officer alone.','The student’s friend.','The admissions team reviews the evidence.')],
'Guide a student with a conditional offer through the difference between acceptance and registration.',
'An international student asks whether they can attend classes immediately after receiving an offer. Explain what must be verified first.',
'Congratulations on your offer. However, an offer and enrollment are separate stages. Please check whether any conditions are still outstanding and follow the university’s registration instructions. The admissions team can confirm your final status.',
'Write a friendly, accurate reply to a student who uploaded the wrong file in the admissions portal.',
'Dear Student, Thank you for your message. The document currently shown in your application does not appear to match the required final academic record. Please review the instructions in the admissions portal and upload the correct file through the approved channel. The admissions team will review your submission and update your status. Kind regards, Academic Support',
('If the document will arrive, we will review it.','If the document arrives, we will review it.','Use Present Simple in the if-clause of a first conditional.'),
Q('A student requests a guarantee of admission. Which reply is responsible?','I can explain the requirements; the admissions team confirms decisions.','I guarantee approval if you email me.','All applicants are automatically accepted.','Guidance is not a final admissions decision.')))
LESSONS.append(make(
8,'Academic Calendar & Scheduling','Академический календарь и расписание','scheduling, availability and changes',
'''semester | семестр | One of the main academic periods in a year. | The autumn semester begins in September.
academic calendar | академический календарь | The official schedule of key academic dates. | Check the academic calendar before confirming a deadline.
timetable | расписание занятий | A schedule of classes or activities. | The timetable shows the new seminar room.
availability | доступное время | Times when someone is free to meet. | Could you share your availability next week?
reschedule | перенести время | To arrange a different time for an existing event. | We need to reschedule the orientation session.
time slot | временной интервал | A specific period available for an activity. | The 2:00 p.m. slot is still open.
conflict | пересечение; конфликт в расписании | Two commitments that cannot happen together. | There is a conflict between the lab and the seminar.
postpone | отложить | To move an event to a later time. | The briefing was postponed until Friday.
confirm attendance | подтвердить участие | To state that someone will participate. | Please confirm attendance by Wednesday.
notify participants | уведомить участников | To inform everyone affected by an update. | We must notify participants about the room change.''',
'''Would Tuesday afternoon work for you? | Вам подойдёт вторник после обеда?
I can offer two alternative time slots. | Я могу предложить два других временных интервала.
There seems to be a scheduling conflict. | Похоже, возникло пересечение в расписании.
The session has been moved to another room. | Занятие перенесено в другую аудиторию.
Could you confirm your availability? | Не могли бы вы подтвердить, когда вы свободны?
I\'ll send the revised timetable shortly. | Я скоро отправлю обновлённое расписание.''',
'Finding a workable workshop time',
'''A programme team is arranging a workshop with lecturers from two departments and a visiting international specialist. The first proposed time is Wednesday at 3:00 p.m., but one lecturer has a compulsory class at that hour. Another participant is joining online from a different time zone. The Academic Support officer must find a practical solution without assuming that everyone\'s calendar follows the same working day.
The officer checks the academic calendar to avoid examination periods and other institutional events. Then they offer two time slots and ask participants to confirm their availability. The proposed dates are written with the weekday, full date and local time zone. This detail is especially important when coordinating internationally; “Wednesday at three” may be understood differently by people in different locations.
Once most participants confirm, the officer checks with the event organiser before treating the arrangement as final. The updated invitation includes the meeting link, duration, room details and a contact for technical difficulties. Participants receive a clear note about what changed. A good scheduling email is short but complete. It states the original issue, gives a confirmed alternative and makes the action required from the recipient obvious. If attendance is not yet confirmed, the officer says the time is proposed rather than final. Small distinctions like these prevent unnecessary confusion.''',
'''Lecturer | I have a compulsory seminar at the proposed workshop time.
Officer | Thanks for letting me know. Would Thursday at 11:00 or Friday at 14:00 work for you?
Lecturer | Thursday is better. Is the visiting specialist available then?
Officer | I have not confirmed their time zone yet, so the slot is still provisional.
Lecturer | Understood. Can you check before sending the invitation?
Officer | Absolutely. I\'ll confirm with the specialist and organiser first.
Lecturer | Please include the online meeting link in the update.
Officer | Of course. I\'ll send the revised invitation once the time is confirmed.''',
('Future arrangements: will vs going to','Будущие действия и договорённости','Use will for a promise or decision made now, going to for a plan, and Present Continuous for a fixed arrangement. Label provisional times clearly.','I will check with the organiser.;We are going to review the timetable.;We are meeting on Friday at 14:00.','Avoid saying “The meeting is confirmed” before necessary people agree.',Q('Which statement is an immediate promise?','I will check the organiser’s availability.','I checked yesterday, maybe.','We is meeting on Friday.','Will often expresses a promise made in the moment.')),
[Q('Why should the invitation name a time zone?','International participants may be in different locations.','Every university uses the same time.','Time zones only matter for exams.','Time-zone clarity prevents misunderstandings.'),Q('When is a slot described as provisional?','Before all required confirmations are received.','After it has been cancelled.','Only when a room number is unknown.','Provisional means not yet final.')],
[Q('Which alternative does the lecturer prefer?','Thursday at 11:00.','Friday at 14:00.','Wednesday at 15:00.','The lecturer says Thursday is better.'),Q('What must be checked before sending the new invitation?','The visiting specialist and organiser.','The weather forecast.','The lecturer’s passport.','The officer promises to confirm availability first.')],
'Coordinate a workshop with two lecturers and one participant in another time zone.',
'A professor cannot attend a meeting. Offer two alternatives and explain which details still need confirmation.',
'Thank you for letting me know. We could meet on Thursday at 11:00 or Friday at 14:00 local time. Both times are provisional until I confirm the visitor’s availability. Once everyone agrees, I will send the updated invitation and meeting link.',
'Write an email proposing a new workshop time because of a scheduling conflict. Include full date, time zone and request for confirmation.',
'Dear Colleagues, We have identified a scheduling conflict with the original workshop slot. Could you please confirm whether Thursday, 12 November, at 11:00 (Tashkent time, UTC+5) would be convenient? The date is provisional until all participants confirm. Once finalised, I will send the revised invitation and link. Best regards, Academic Support',
('We will meeting next Thursday.','We are meeting next Thursday.','Use Present Continuous with be + -ing for a fixed arrangement.'),
Q('A partner is in another country. How should you propose a time?','Include the full date, time and time zone, and request confirmation.','Write only “tomorrow at three”.','Assume their time zone matches yours.','Complete scheduling details avoid ambiguity.')))
